<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nabopolassar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/626-bce">626</a>–605 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century-bce">7th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nabopolassar was a Chaldean leader who seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> in 626 BCE and founded the Neo-Babylonian or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaldean-empire">Chaldean Empire</a>. He took advantage of succession disputes in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a> after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashurbanipal">Ashurbanipal</a>'s death and allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyaxares">Cyaxares of Media</a>. Their coalition overwhelmed the Assyrian Empire's heartland in the 610s BCE, and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nebuchadnezzar-ii">Nebuchadnezzar II</a> succeeded him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nabopolassar.jpg" alt="Nabopolassar" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Osama Shukir Muhammed Amin FRCP(Glasg) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cylinder_of_Nabopolassar_from_Babylon,_Mesopotamia..JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Nabopolassar's origins are unclear. In his own inscriptions he calls himself a son of a nobody, a descriptor known from no other Mesopotamian king. The later kings <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neriglissar">Neriglissar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nabonidus">Nabonidus</a> also had no blood tie to earlier royalty but named their fathers with pride. Historians have variously identified him as a Chaldean, an Assyrian or a Babylonian. Near-contemporary texts such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk">Uruk</a> prophecy call him king of the sea, meaning southernmost <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonia">Babylonia</a>, and a letter of the Assyrian king Sinsharishkun calls him a man from the lower sea. The term Chaldean dynasty remains in common use for the family he founded.</p><p>In the middle of the seventh century BCE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neo-assyrian-empire">Neo-Assyrian Empire</a> ruled the entire <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/near-east">Near East</a>. Babylonia was divided among native <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonians">Babylonians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaldean-tribes">Chaldean tribes</a> and Arameans, and the Assyrian king Tiglath-Pileser III had conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> in 729 BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Nabopolassar probably held high office in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk">Uruk</a> before he rebelled against Sinsharishkun in early 626 BCE. He took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nippur">Nippur</a> from their Assyrian garrisons. In October 626 BCE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyrians</a> retook Nippur and besieged both Uruk and Babylon, but Nabopolassar repelled both sieges. He was crowned king of Babylon on 22 or 23 November 626 BCE. Fighting for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonia">Babylonia</a> lasted about ten years, and Sinsharishkun recaptured Uruk in 623 BCE.</p><p>In 616 BCE Nabopolassar marched up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> into Assyrian lands, took the city of Hindanu and defeated an Assyrian force near Gablinu. Pharaoh <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/psamtik-i">Psamtik I</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> aided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a>, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonians">Babylonians</a> kept the middle Euphrates. In May 615 BCE they attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assur">Assur</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medes">Medes</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyaxares">Cyaxares</a> entered Assyria soon after, and in 614 BCE sacked Assur. In 612 BCE the Medes and Babylonians took and burned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nineveh">Nineveh</a>, and Sinsharishkun probably died defending it. Babylonian chronicles lament the sackings, and Nabopolassar's own inscriptions sometimes ascribe the victory to the gods and sometimes glory in the destruction.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/necho-ii">Pharaoh Necho II</a> fought for several years to restore <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a> until his defeat at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-carchemish">Battle of Carchemish</a> in 605 BCE. Nabopolassar died that year and was succeeded by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nebuchadnezzar-ii">Nebuchadnezzar II</a>. Babylon, neglected during the wars, needed a new wall and restored temples such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/esagila">Esagila</a>. Babylonians long remembered him as the avenger of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akkad">Akkad</a>, and Hellenistic authors, who called him Belesys or Bupolasaros, cast him as a pious and just king ordered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marduk">Marduk</a> to free his homeland.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nabopolassar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It failed at last against the Medo-Babylonian coalition of the 610s BCE, when the combined forces of Cyaxares the Mede and <b>Nabopolassar of Babylon</b> overwhelmed even Assyria's fortified heartland.</blockquote><a href="/empire/assyrian-empire">Assyrian Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Nabopolassar</b>, a leader of uncertain origin traditionally regarded as a Chaldean chieftain, seized Babylon in 626 BCE amid the turmoil that followed the death of the Assyrian king Ashurbanipal.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chaldean-empire">Chaldean Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In alliance with <b>Nabopolassar of Babylon</b> he destroyed Assyria, taking Ashur in 614 BCE and Nineveh in 612 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/median-empire">Median Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In the dedication inscription the king calls himself the first born son of <b>Nabopolassar</b>, King of Babylon, and says that the two gate entrances of the city walls, Imgur-Ellil and Nemetti-Ellil, had become increasingly lower after the street from Babylon was filled.</blockquote><a href="/work/ishtar-gate">Ishtar Gate · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>After the death of Ashurbanipal a struggle over the succession weakened the state, Babylon rose under <b>Nabopolassar</b>, and the Medes and Babylonians took Assur in 614, sacked Nineveh in 612, and defeated the last Assyrian attempt to retake Harran in 609.</blockquote><a href="/essays/the-collapse-problem">The Collapse Problem</a></li><li><blockquote>The Babylonian Chronicle records that <b>Nabopolassar</b> of the Chaldean Empire took Suhu in 616 BCE as he advanced against Assyria, ending its separate identity.</blockquote><a href="/state/land-of-suhu">Land of Suhu</a></li></ul></section>
